---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StatusBadge from "../components/StatusBadge.astro";
import { ROOT } from "../lib/data.ts";
import { REPO_URL, STATUS_HELP } from "../lib/config.ts";
import { STATUSES, wantedIssueUrl, ODB_REPO_URL, REPORT_TEMPLATE } from "@opendevicedb/core";

const example = readFileSync(join(ROOT, "data/devices/ryze/tello.yaml"), "utf8");
const claimExample = `claims:
  - who: Jane Doe
    contact: https://github.com/janedoe
    scope: BLE control protocol
    since: 2026-10-17
    expires: 2026-11-16
    agent: claude-code`;
const verificationExample = `verification:
  - method: hardware-replay
    by: Jane Doe
    date: 2026-11-10
    firmware: "1.4.2"
    notes: tests/replay_takeoff_land.py passes on two units`;
const partialExample = `interfaces:
  - name: BLE light control
    transport: ble
    status: partial
    confidence: low
    notes: >-
      Commands go to characteristic 0000ffe1 (service 0000ffe0), 7-byte frames
      starting 0x7e, ending 0xef; byte 3 = brightness 0-100. Seen in captures,
      not replayed yet.`;
const installExample = `install:
  - project: Tasmota
    method: web-serial          # pairing, ota-exploit, vendor-upload, usb-bootloader, sd-card, serial, spi-programmer…
    guide: https://tasmota.github.io/docs/Getting-Started/
    installer: https://tasmota.github.io/install/
    difficulty: medium          # easy: no tools, stays closed · medium: cable or command line · hard: opening, soldering
    tools: [USB cable]
    opens_device: false
    mains_powered: false
    brick_risk: low             # none, low, medium, high
    reversible: partial         # yes, no, partial
    revert: https://example.org/back-to-stock
    firmware_constraints: Units sold after mid-2023 ship another chip and cannot be flashed this way
    notes: Hold the button while plugging in the cable.`;
const freedomExample = `projects:
  - name: LineageOS 22.2
    url: https://wiki.lineageos.org/devices/bluejay/
    kind: firmware
    freedom:
      nonfree: [vendor-blobs, modem, gpu]   # device-firmware, wifi, bluetooth, camera, sensors, bootloader, other; [] = nothing known
      cloud: none                           # setup-only, optional, required, unknown
      notes: Proprietary files are extracted from the stock firmware`;
const reportFormUrl = `${ODB_REPO_URL}/issues/new?template=${REPORT_TEMPLATE}`;
const themes = { light: "github-light", dark: "github-dark" } as const;
---
<Base title="Contribute" description="How to add or improve a device record, claim a device you are working on, and how verification works.">
  <div class="article">
    <h1>Contribute</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The database is a set of YAML files in git. Everything — new devices, findings, claims —
      arrives as a pull request, by hand or through the <a href="/agents/">MCP server</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="workflow">Pull request workflow</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Fork <a href={REPO_URL}>the repository</a> (or use "Edit this record on GitHub" on any device page).</li>
      <li>Add or edit <code>data/devices/&lt;vendor&gt;/&lt;model&gt;.yaml</code>. The id is <code>&lt;vendor-slug&gt;/&lt;model-slug&gt;</code>, lowercase <code>a-z 0-9 -</code>.</li>
      <li>Run <code>npm install &amp;&amp; npm run validate</code>: every record is checked against <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/schema/device.schema.json`}><code>schema/device.schema.json</code></a>.</li>
      <li>Open the pull request. Say what you checked yourself and what an AI tool produced.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A curated file overrides an imported record with the same id: to enrich a device that came from
      another database, create its curated file — device pages for imported records link to a prefilled one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="format">Record format</h2>
    <p>A real record, <code>data/devices/ryze/tello.yaml</code>:</p>
    <Code code={example} lang="yaml" themes={themes} />
    <p class="small muted">The <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/SPEC.md`}>specification</a> describes every field; the JSON Schema is normative.</p>

    <h2 id="partial">Small findings are welcome</h2>
    <p>
      You don't need a full protocol or a working tool to contribute. A single useful fact — the BLE
      service that carries commands, the checksum a packet uses, the UART pins and baud rate, the chip
      under the shield, a capture of the pairing — can save the next person (or agent) hours. Add it as
      an interface with status <code>partial</code> and an honest <code>confidence</code>: maintainers check
      that it is relevant and plausible before merging, and the record says how far it was verified.
    </p>
    <Code code={partialExample} lang="yaml" themes={themes} />
    <h2 id="status">Status levels</h2>
    <ul class="list-plain">
      {STATUSES.map((s) => <li><StatusBadge status={s} /> <span class="muted">— {STATUS_HELP[s]}</span></li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="claims">Claims: say you are on it</h2>
    <p>
      Before you start reverse engineering a device, add a claim. It shows the device as
      <StatusBadge status="in-progress" /> on the site and in agents' lookups, with your contact,
      so nobody starts the same job in parallel. Claims expire (90 days at most); renew or publish.
    </p>
    <Code code={claimExample} lang="yaml" themes={themes} />
    <p>Agents do this with the <code>claim_device</code> tool.</p>

    <h2 id="wanted">Requests: say you want a device</h2>
    <p>
      Can't find a device, or nothing usable exists for it yet? <a href={wantedIssueUrl({})}>Request it</a>
      with the issue form (vendor, model, identifiers, what you want to do with it). Others who want the same device
      add a 👍 to that issue instead of opening a new one; duplicates are pointed to the first request and closed.
      The <a href="/wanted/">Wanted</a> page ranks requests by interest, so people who can reverse engineer
      know where their work helps most. A request closes by itself, with a link, once a usable result
      (at least <StatusBadge status="partial-protocol" />) reaches the directory.
    </p>
    <p>Requests are public GitHub issues and follow the <a href="/policy/">content policy</a>. Agents use the <code>request_device</code> tool, which only builds the link: the person opens it.</p>

    <h2 id="install">Install routes and reports</h2>
    <p>
      A record can list the ways to put its open firmware or software on the device, under <code>install</code>.
      Device pages show them as a "Get started" block, easiest first, with the official guide or installer.
      A route always points to the project's own instructions (<code>guide</code> is required): nothing is flashed
      or hosted here. Set the safety flags honestly; a device that is mains-powered and must be opened only gets a
      warning and a plain link to the guide.
    </p>
    <Code code={installExample} lang="yaml" themes={themes} />
    <p class="small muted">The values above are an illustration of the format, not a real device's route.</p>
    <p>
      Tried a route? <a href={reportFormUrl}>Report how it went</a> — worked, partly or failed, with the firmware
      version you started from and the project version you installed. Device pages have a "Report how it went" link
      on each route that fills in the device and route for you. Reports are public GitHub issues labelled
      <code>install-report</code>; the site counts them per route ("3 worked, 1 failed") and publishes them in
      <a href="/api/install-reports.json"><code>/api/install-reports.json</code></a>. They never change a record:
      a maintainer may turn a report into a <code>manual</code> verification entry by hand.
    </p>

    <h2 id="freedom">Freedom: what stays non-free</h2>
    <p>
      Running a free OS or firmware does not make a device fully free. Each project can say, under <code>freedom</code>,
      what still needs proprietary code once it is in use (<code>nonfree</code>) and whether the device still needs the
      vendor's cloud (<code>cloud</code>). Take the values from the project's own documentation, never guess:
      leave <code>freedom</code> out when you don't know, and write <code>nonfree: []</code> only when nothing non-free
      is known to remain. Device pages show it next to each project, and the directory's "No cloud" filter uses it.
    </p>
    <Code code={freedomExample} lang="yaml" themes={themes} />
    <p class="small muted">The values above illustrate the format. Imported sources set only what holds for every device they list (e.g. Zigbee2MQTT: the stock firmware stays, no cloud); a curated record refines it per device.</p>

    <h2 id="verification">Verification levels</h2>
    <p>
      AI-produced protocol descriptions can be confidently wrong. Each interface carries a
      <code>confidence</code> (<code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>high</code>), and each record a <code>verification</code> list:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong><code>hardware-replay</code></strong> — a replayable test (script, capture replay) was run on a real device, with the firmware version stated. Only this earns the <span class="badge verified">Verified on hardware</span> badge.</li>
      <li><strong><code>manual</code></strong> — someone tried it by hand on a real device.</li>
      <li><strong><code>none</code></strong> — not verified; desk research or captures only.</li>
    </ul>
    <Code code={verificationExample} lang="yaml" themes={themes} />
    <p>Set <code>provenance.ai_assisted: true</code> whenever an AI tool wrote part of the record, and list the tools.</p>

    <h2 id="policy">What we accept</h2>
    <p>
      Protocol descriptions, original open-source code and links — never firmware images, vendor code
      or keys, and nothing that defeats a safety function (Remote ID, geofencing, radio limits…).
      Read the <a href="/policy/">content policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="news">News posts</h2>
    <p>Add <code>news/YYYY-MM-DD-slug.md</code> with <code>title</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>summary</code> and <code>tags</code> in the frontmatter.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
